  "summary_text": "Kyle, yesterday was mixed: Port Washington ran lean on capacity but delivered strong unit economics, while Hicksville held steady operationally but showed early August booking softness. Neither location has a real performance problem\u2014both are tracking within calendar expectations\u2014but you need to restore groomer coverage immediately.\n\nPort Washington: Three groomers yesterday delivered $525 per groomer-day, 6% above your trailing 30-day baseline, so the floor executed well. But transaction count dropped 13% and retail cratered 21%\u2014the retail miss is real and worth investigating (front-of-house upsell discipline, or customer traffic composition?). More importantly, you're only scheduled for zero groomers today and five tomorrow after running three yesterday. MTD you're at 3 groomer-days versus 6 last month; this is the capacity constraint eating into August's top line. The week-to-date jump (up 22% appointments) suggests pent-up demand or a scheduling correction\u2014protect that momentum with consistent groomer availability next week.\n\nHicksville: Two groomers yesterday, $425 per groomer-day (above baseline), and you're only down 0.8% to DOW-adjusted expectation, so August's 20% revenue dip versus July is structural calendar drift, not operational failure. Add-on revenue collapsed 60%, though\u2014that's a signal to watch upsell discipline. You have zero bathers scheduled today and tomorrow, and only 18 groomer-days across the next week; this location is materially understaffed relative to demand.\n\nPort Washington has $296k in dormant/lapsed customer value and Hicksville $91k\u2014both pools merit reactivation campaigns once you stabilize capacity.\n\n**Today: zero groomer-days across both stores (Port Washington bathers 7h, Hicksville 0h); tomorrow: 6 groomer-days total (Port Washington 5, Hicksville 1); next 7 days: 52 groomer-days scheduled.**"
